*-*-* Here's the text of the feature. *-*-*

(Game Informer, May 2007, page 102)

Mod World

NETHACK
http://www.nethack.org

(<picture of a typical dungeon floor>)

It doesn't get a whole lot more historic than NetHack, the modern
descendant of Rogue, the ASCII-based dungeon crawler that was spawned
on UNIX-based university mainframes back in 1985. The game has grown
and evolved over the years, and is still supported by a dedicated
group of programmers who lurk in the shadows of the INternet.
Knockoffs like the Mystery Dungeon series or Izuna the Unemployed
Ninja are kiddie pools compared to the shark-infested (but totally
awesome) waters of NetHack. Turn by turn, you descend into the
dungeon.

(<picture of a dungeon floor with tiles> caption: "Several graphical
interfaces for NetHack exist, but they're for wusses")

Contending with hunger, encumbrance, and deadly traps can be more
engrossing and difficult than fending off the monsters that inhabit
the depths. Nearly everything can be done in NetHack, provided you can
find the command to do it -- for example, there is a whole set of
rules governing the inscriptio0n of the word "Elbereth" (see Tolkien's
writings for its meaning) as a protective ward. NetHack is not for the
faint of heart. You'll

(<picture of another typical dungeon floor>)

die -- repeatedly. You'll read guides on the 'Net, post on message
boards, pore over wiki entries, and keep dying. The joke is that your
character is erased from your PC as soon as that fateful turn is
complete; there's no such thing as a checkpoint in NetHack. You'll
just have to suck it up, learn from the experience, and try again.
Once you get a handle on the game, though, this little ASCII dungeon
simulation can be one of the deepest, richest experiences around.



